Eitri @VERSION@ -- Sick of reading agent replies and Markdown in a terminal? Run your own Neovim in a GUI that renders them.
Built from commit @COMMIT@ in the Ubuntu 24.04 release container. It runs on glibc 2.39, GTK 4.14
and WebKitGTK 2.40 (the webkitgtk-6.0 API) or newer.

0.4.0 is for running several agents next to your own Neovim. Every window shares what its agents are doing, so you can
see which one waits for you and go to it with one key. Claude tabs get plan mode, pasted images, your open buffers as
context and vim-style keys in the panel, and permission cards are answered in a mode of their own. On Hyprland, sway
and niri, chats and pages become windows your compositor tiles. A Neovim already running in a terminal can be moved
into an Eitri window, and Eitri reopens your files where you left them. A window can also work on a project that
lives on another Linux machine, over ssh (experimental, off by default). Your Neovim stays the editor: a real
`nvim --embed` with your own `init.lua`, plugins, LSP and keymaps.

## What's new since 0.3.0

**Many agents at once**

- Every Eitri window now shares one row per agent tab (who it is, its state, since when, whether it waits for you), and
  three bundled pages read them: Strips (`agents-strips`), a sidebar of who needs you that stays hidden until
  `<prefix> U` opens it; The pass (`agents-pass`), one line that shows only while an agent waits; and a `top`-style
  table (`agents-top`). They are off until `init.lua` loads them, for example `eitri.plugin.load("agents-strips")` or
  `eitri.plugin.load("agents-top", { key = "A" })`. If something of yours already uses `U`, it keeps the key and Strips
  has none.
- `<prefix> u` goes to the agent that has waited longest for you, in whichever window it is, and on to the next;
  `<prefix> B` goes back to where you started. Neither answers anything: you answer in the chat, as before.
- `eitri agents` prints the same rows in a terminal (`--json`, `--watch` for a status bar), and a page you write can
  read them through the new `agents` capability.
- The layer is on by default; `eitri.config.set("agents.enabled", false)` turns it off for a window. The guide's new
  "Many agents at once" page has the details.
- Removed `eitri-supervisor` and `EITRI_SUPERVISOR`. If you used them, load `agents-top` (above) for the same overview
  inside the window, or run `eitri agents --watch`.

**Windows your window manager tiles**

- On Hyprland, sway and niri, each chat view and each page now opens as a window of its own and your compositor tiles
  it. The editor stays in the first window, with one bottom terminal. `window.tiling` chooses: `"auto"`, the default,
  does this when the compositor answers within 500 ms, `"compositor"` does it on any Wayland desktop, and `"eitri"`
  keeps everything inside Eitri's window. GNOME, KDE and X11 see no change under `"auto"`.
- The first start in this mode says so once, in a toast: `Hyprland detected: chats and pages open as their own windows;
  window.tiling = "eitri" keeps them inside`. On those three compositors the first-start screen offers it as a preset.
- Chat windows have the app id `cn.huntergrey.eitri.Panel`, page windows `cn.huntergrey.eitri.Page`, and titles that
  never change, so a window rule can place them; a rule's placement wins over what Eitri remembers. The guide's new
  "Compositor windows" page has a rule for each compositor.
- `:EitriTutor` follows the mode: in a window that opened in compositor mode it teaches that mode's keys (chat windows,
  the bottom terminal, moving out to your compositor) instead of Eitri's own layout.
- A finished turn in another window no longer shows as running, in the top bar or in the close question.
- Tried on sway 1.12, Hyprland 0.57.0 (Lua config) and 0.56.2 (`hyprland.conf` and Lua config) and niri 26.04; not
  yet on GNOME or KDE. Each chat window costs 51.4 MiB more than
  the same chat as a tile with four projects open (release build, medians). The page's "Known gaps" lists what does not
  work yet.

**Your Neovim, moved in, reopened or kept beside**

- `:EitriOpen` in a Neovim running in a terminal moves that same Neovim into an Eitri window, with the agent panel
  beside it and the bottom terminal under it. Nothing restarts: buffers, unsaved changes, jobs and `:terminal` stay.
  The terminal waits at a short screen, and the Neovim goes back to it when you close the window, press `Enter` in the
  terminal, log out, or Eitri dies. Eitri never signals your Neovim and never quits it. `eitri open --nvim <address>`
  does the same from a shell.
- It needs Neovim 0.12 or newer in that terminal and the eitri.nvim plugin for the command. It refuses with one line,
  and leaves the terminal as it was, when another UI is attached to that Neovim, when it is waiting for a key or busy,
  or when its address is not a local socket of yours (no remote servers). `editor.takeover = false` turns it off.
- The Markdown preview, or any page, can open as a window of its own beside your own Neovim: `:EitriPage markdown`, or
  `eitri page markdown --nvim <address>`. It follows that Neovim's buffer; `Enter` on a block moves Neovim's cursor
  there.
- Starting a project again brings back what you had open: the files in their splits and tab pages, each window at its
  line and scroll position, the listed buffers, and which window held the cursor. Eitri keeps a small record of file
  names and positions (never text), updated every 30 seconds, when the window loses focus and when the editor exits, so
  a crash costs about the last 30 seconds. It stands aside when a session plugin is installed (persistence.nvim,
  auto-session, resession.nvim and the others the guide lists), with `eitri FILE`, with `--clean`, in the second
  window and beside your own Neovim. `editor.restore = false` turns it off.
- Quitting Neovim from a start screen (LazyVim's dashboard, alpha, mini.starter and the like) while the window holds
  more than the editor now asks `close Eitri? (y/n)`: one `q` too many after closing Lazy's window no longer closes
  all of Eitri. `:qa`, `:wqa` and `ZZ` from your files still close at once.
- If Neovim itself is killed or crashes, the window stays and says so, and `<prefix> e` starts it again. A start that
  finds unsaved changes in swap files says so and recovers nothing until you ask.
- A crash of Eitri leaves `crash-<time>-<pid>.log` under `~/.local/state/eitri/log/`, readable by you only, and the
  next start says so once with the path. It holds the version, the build, where the code stopped, a backtrace and the
  last lines the window printed, with no prompt, conversation or tool output. Eitri sends nothing: you attach it to a
  bug report if you want to. A crash inside GTK, WebKit or a graphics driver writes no record.

**Opening a project**

- `<prefix> O` lays a list of your other projects over the window, the one you used last first, with a line to filter
  it or to type a path (`~/src/app`, `Tab` completes it). `Enter` takes you there: a project that has a window comes
  forward, any other opens in a new window. The same list opens from `:Eitri project.choose`, a click on the project's
  name in the top bar, and `p` on an empty chat tab, in the chat and page windows of compositor mode too.
- The list takes the keys of the session list: `j` and `k`, `gg` and `G`, `Enter`, `/` for the line, `~` to browse
  from home, `Esc` or `q` to close.
- You can walk to a project Eitri has not opened before: `h` on a project shows the folder that holds it, `l` or
  `Enter` goes into a folder, `h` goes up, and `Enter` on the `.` row opens the folder you are in. Git repositories,
  links and mount points are marked, and a network share that does not answer says so within two seconds while the
  rest of the disk stays browsable.
- Starting Eitri from the desktop with no file or folder no longer opens your home folder: it shows the same list in a
  small window first, with no Neovim and no trust question. `window.desktop_start = "last"` opens the project you used
  most recently instead, and `"cwd"` brings back the old behaviour.
- A window started from a launcher or a key binding asks your login shell once (at most 2 seconds) for its `PATH`, so
  the editor, the bottom terminal and the agents find what your shell configuration adds, and it finds `claude` and
  `codex` where their installers put them. `EITRI_LOGIN_PATH=off` skips the shell.
- `eitri` in a terminal gives the prompt back as soon as the window is up and prints nothing; the window keeps running
  when you close that terminal, and its output goes to a log under `~/.local/state/eitri/log/`. `eitri --no-fork`
  keeps the window in the foreground.

**Remote development** (experimental)

- `eitri host open example-host:/path/to/project` opens a project that lives on another Linux machine. The agent and
  its tools run on the server, next to the code and its toolchains; your Neovim, its plugins, the panel and your keys
  stay on your computer, which keeps a mirror of the project at the same path. Your saves travel to the server, and
  the agent's edits come back.
- In the window's Neovim, git reads run on your copy and git writes (`commit`, `checkout`, `push` and the others) run
  on the server, after your saves have reached it. The bottom terminal of a remote window is a shell on the server; it
  keeps running when the link drops, and the window attaches to it again.
- Permission cards on a remote tab follow the same rules as on a local project: Eitri's helper checks each path the
  agent names on the server's disk (inside the project or not, a link that leads out, whether the folder is a
  boundary at all) and checks again just before Eitri answers a request by itself. When the server cannot check paths
  for a tab (a tab you just moved to another window, an agent started by an older helper), the tab says so and asks
  about every file and command. Turn review is not available on remote tabs yet.
- The session chooser (`<prefix> w`) of a remote window lists the sessions Claude Code recorded on the server for the
  project, those you started with `claude` in a terminal there included, and continues one with its history read
  from the server.
- Eitri's helper keeps a tab's history on the server for two days after its agent ends there, and drops it sooner
  when the helper's files pass 1 GiB or that disk has less than 1 GiB free. Reopening a tab whose missed history was
  dropped offers, while Claude Code's transcript is still on the server, to rebuild the tab's history from it,
  stopping the agent there first if it still runs.
- Language servers run on the server, next to the project's toolchain, with no change to your Neovim configuration,
  and see your unsaved text as you type. A closed lid or a dropped connection does not stop them: the server keeps
  one for `remote_dev.lsp_grace` seconds (30 minutes by default) and Neovim reconnects to it, so nothing is indexed
  again. `gd` into a file that sync does not carry (the standard library, a dependency) opens it read-only from the
  server, and `eitri host exec` runs a formatter, a linter or `make` on the server and brings back what it changed.
- None of that needs an Eitri window: a Neovim in an ordinary terminal with the `eitri.nvim` plugin gets the same
  language servers, `gd` and exec wrappers, and `:checkhealth eitri` says what it found. `:EitriRemote term` opens a
  shell on the server in a Neovim tab.
- It is off by default: `eitri.config.set("remote_dev.enabled", true)` turns it on. You need key-based ssh, OpenSSH
  8.5 or later and Mutagen 0.18 or later on your computer (Eitri does not ship Mutagen), and `git` and `claude`,
  installed and set up, on the server. The guide's new "Remote development" page has the details.

**Your editor and your agent**

- An agent in any tab (Claude Code, Codex or one you registered) gets editor tools: `open_file`, `read_buffer`,
  `editor_state`, `diagnostics`, `usages`, `hover`, `symbols`, `lsp_rename`, `format`, `lsp_code_actions`,
  `apply_code_action` and `apply_proposal`. They show a file beside your window without taking your cursor, read
  buffers as your editor holds them (unsaved changes included), and ask the language servers already running there.
  A rename, a format or a code action only makes a proposal; `apply_proposal` writes it, and in a Manual or an Auto
  tab that is a card. Asking about a file outside the project is a card too, and so is loading a file that is not open
  in Manual. `nvim_exec` stays off until you turn it on and is always a card. `agent.editor.tools = false` turns the
  set off, and `agent.editor.tool.<name>` turns one off.
- Turning on `agent.editor.follow`, or `<leader>e` in a tab, shows each completed edit in the editor at its first
  changed line. It is off by default and never moves your cursor.
- In Visual mode, `<leader>ae` puts the selection in the chat's box with `Edit the selection: ` ready, so the change
  comes back as an ordinary edit card.
- In a Claude tab, three names beyond files work after the same at-sign that completes file names: `buffers` gives
  Claude the project's open files as they are on screen, `quickfix` the quickfix list and `diagnostics` what your
  language servers said at that moment. Long ones stop at a bound (1000 lines and 32 KiB per file, 96 KiB for all
  buffers, 200 list entries), and the message says where. `!` at the start of the box runs the rest as your own shell
  command in the project, and its output goes to Claude, as in the terminal.
- With `review.live = true` (off by default), a file the agent edits is drawn in your editor as the edit lands and again
  when the turn ends: added lines highlighted, removed lines shown as virtual text. `<localleader>a` keeps the drawing
  and `<localleader>d` undoes it in that buffer as one step, `]h` and `[h` move between hunks. It draws only over a
  buffer you have not modified and never moves your cursor. In turn review, a changed line now also marks the words
  that changed in it.
- `terminal.claude_ide = true` (off by default) lets a `claude` you run in the bottom terminal attach to the window's
  editor for diffs, diagnostics and selections. It was built against Claude Code 2.1.311's protocol and tested with a
  stand-in client, not with a real interactive `claude`.

**Day to day in a Claude tab**

- `<leader>M` chooses the model and `<leader>E` the effort (also a click on the band, or a bare `/model` or `/effort`),
  also while a turn runs: the running response finishes on the old model and the next request uses the new one.
  `/model <name>` and `/effort <level>` set one at once and are never sent as text. A registered ACP agent's tab offers
  the models and efforts that agent lists; a Codex tab offers neither.
- During a turn, a message you send steers it as before, a slash command waits for the turn's end, and `Ctrl+x` then
  `Enter` sends the draft after the turn.
- When `claude` is not found, the panel and the first-start screen say where to get Claude Code.
- Agent replies draw closed `mermaid` fences as diagrams in the panel (up to 20,000 characters and 500 lines, each in
  a hidden renderer view with a four-second limit). Copying gives the fence's source, and a fence that fails to draw or
  is too large stays as code. Claude is told that the panel draws flowcharts, sequence and class diagrams.
- A reply's separate text blocks keep their paragraph break instead of running together.
- Tables in a reply stay readable in a narrow panel: code in a cell can break after `/` and after the `,` of a `{a,b}`, a column of CJK
  text is no longer squeezed to two characters, and a table that still does not fit is drawn as a list,
  one `Header: value` line per cell.
- Reopening a project whose chat you were in shows the conversation's earlier messages within about a second, while
  the tab is still starting. A message you type meanwhile is sent once the session is back, and stays in the box if
  it does not come back.
- A tab keeps about 200 settled turns in the panel (`agent.history_turns`, 10 to 2000). Older turns are unloaded, a line
  says `N earlier turns not loaded.`, `Enter` on it (or `:history older`) loads an older page, and `:history latest`
  goes back. This changes only what the panel holds: Claude Code's own session file is never touched. Resuming an ACP
  session keeps its last 200 turns and says how many it left out.
- The trust question shows what changed in `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/rules/` and the other instruction files since you
  last answered, and covers `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json` and rules in the directories above the repository.
- A Codex or ACP tab shows its own activity mark (a softly pulsing bullet) and the agent's name, and says it is waiting
  for its agent after 10 seconds, not that a sidecar is building.

**The panel with vim keys**

- Answering a card moved to **card mode**: `Alt-n` or `Enter` on a waiting card takes the keys, then `1`, `2` or `3`
  answer in Claude Code's order (1 Approve, 2 Always allow where a rule can be saved, 3 Deny), or `h`/`l` and `Enter`.
  A digit answers only after a short arming time, and a letter never answers.
- `y` is an operator: `yy` the row, `yim` the message's Markdown, `yio` a tool's whole output, `yic` its input, `yih`
  a hunk, and `y` with a motion copies several rows. `?` searches backward, `Ctrl+o` and `Ctrl+i` walk the places you
  jumped from, and `]t`, `]d`, `]m`, `]c` (with `[`) step between tool calls, errors, edits and hunks.
- `gf` opens the path under the caret or in a selection and puts labels on every path when there are several. `gd`
  goes to a definition or to the line a path cites, and lands on the definition's column.

**The Markdown preview**

- The preview's cursor is a block on one character, as in Neovim. `j`, `k`, `d`, `u`, `gg`, `G` and the page keys
  move it and the page follows, `h`, `l`, `w`, `b`, `e`, `0` and `$` move it along the text, and `v` starts a selection
  on its character. It takes the colours your `guicursor` gives Normal mode, and blinks only when that entry sets
  blink times.
- The preview's text is the editor's size by default (`font_size` overrides it), `{` and `}` jump by block, and a
  selection can take whole table rows. `o` on a diagram or image opens it in a viewer with zathura-like keys (`h j k
  l`, `+` and `-`, `=`, `a` to fit, `Enter` to edit); a diagram stays sharp at any size and redraws while you edit it.
- A held `j` or `k` in the preview scrolls at once and stops when you let go.

**Keys you can find without the guide**

- `review.hint` takes a third value, `"key"`: after a turn that changed files the status band says only
  `c to review`, with no count, nothing above the box, nothing opening. `true` still gives the count and the running
  line; the default stays `false`, nothing.
- While the keys are in the editor, the empty tab says which key brings them to the panel (`Ctrl+l to use these keys`).
- Floats and the scratchpad have plain keys after the prefix: `F` floats a module or puts it back, `_` sends it to
  the scratchpad, `S` shows the scratchpad. The Alt chords still work.
- `<prefix> W` opens the second window, or brings it to the front.
- On sway or i3 with Alt as `$mod`, which takes Eitri's Alt keys after the prefix, Eitri moves them to Ctrl+Alt for
  that window by itself and says so once. `eitri.keymap.use("i3-alt")` keeps the moves; a key of your own stops them.
- The bundled pages (Strips, The pass, the table, the Markdown preview) are listed on the empty tab under "Pages":
  `Enter` loads one into this window for this run, and `o` opens its `init.lua` line in a scratch buffer so you can
  keep it. `:Eitri plugin load <name>` does the same from Neovim. Eitri does not write your `init.lua`.
- After the prefix, Eitri can show a panel of the keys that may follow (`keymap.which_key_delay`, off by default,
  `eitri.config.set("keymap.which_key_delay", 200)` for 200 ms).
- `:Eitri` runs Eitri's window and layout actions from Neovim, and `keymap.nvim_prefix` binds them under your leader.
  `eitri.keymap.set("root", ...)` binds Ctrl and Alt chords of your choice with no prefix; Eitri warns when your input
  method or desktop uses one.
- Text size: `<prefix> Ctrl+=` stays on the pane for as long as Ctrl is down, `Ctrl+0` on the editor returns to the
  `g:neovide_scale_factor` your configuration sets, `Ctrl++` works where `+` is a key of its own (German, Nordic,
  Spanish, Italian), and `text.all.larger`, `.smaller` and `.reset` name the whole-window actions so the two text size
  keys can change places.
- The tmux import reports the split flags it cannot honour, in its notice and in the `?` sheet, and a new guide page,
  Coming from tmux, covers the whole move. A tmux `bind v ...` after the prefix now imports like any other key; `v`
  stays unbound by default.
- The first launch without an `init.lua` offers a key setup and writes a short `init.lua`; it never changes an existing
  one. `Esc` twice, or `d`, and it stops asking; `:Eitri setup` brings it back, and `EITRI_FIRST_START=off` keeps it
  from opening by itself. The screen says which plugin in your Neovim owns, or probably owns, `Ctrl+b` and
  `Ctrl+h/j/k/l`, what `$mod` is under i3 and sway, and whether an input method is running.
- `Ctrl+Space` can be the prefix: a press test checks that it reaches Eitri, and a tmux `prefix C-space` is now read.
  `:EitriTutor` teaches your own prefix.

**The editor**

- On Wayland with GTK 4.24 or newer, unless the graphics driver is NVIDIA's, the editor hands its frames to the
  compositor on a surface of its own, so typing is no longer a refresh behind native Neovide there: a typed letter
  reached the screen 0.02 to 0.46 ms after native Neovide's at the median, on sway and Mutter at 60 and 165 Hz.
  `editor.presentation = "gtk"` keeps the old way; if the compositor refuses the surface, the editor falls back to it
  and prints why. The guide's "How the editor reaches the screen" has the details. It has not been tried on NVIDIA or
  KWin.
- The editor is now Neovide 0.17.1 (it was a snapshot from September, just after 0.16.2). Among the fixes that reach
  Eitri's editor: on Neovim 0.12, rows no longer stay blank after a `:split`, `:update` or `:close` under
  `'lazyredraw'`; a `guifont` that names a font you do not have no longer stops the editor from starting; a plugin that
  replays exit autocommands no longer freezes the quit; and wheel notches that arrive while Neovim is busy queue up,
  each still reaching Neovim as its own scroll. A popup that sits just above the last row (which-key's, for one) no
  longer paints over the statusline.
- Eitri starts the editor's Neovim with `vim.g.snacks_scroll = false`, which turns off the scroll animation of
  snacks.nvim (LazyVim turns it on), since Neovide already animates scrolling and the two together could move the
  cursor around in a Markdown buffer. `vim.g.snacks_scroll = true` in your own configuration brings it back.
- Eitri now needs Neovim 0.11 or newer (it was 0.10). `install.sh` and `eitri setup` offer a private copy of 0.12.6
  when the Neovim on your `PATH` is older. `:NeovideHelp`, which Neovide adds to every Neovim it runs, opens Neovide's
  online documentation, not Eitri's.
- A yank in the editor reaches the system clipboard even when no clipboard tool (wl-clipboard, xclip) is installed.
  Eitri no longer obeys `NEOVIDE_*` variables from your environment in the Linux window, and `Super+q` no longer quits
  the editor.
- A program in the bottom terminal that sets the clipboard with an OSC 52 escape now makes the pane show a notice with
  the terminal's number, which clipboard it set and how many bytes it wrote.

**Permissions**

- An agent's write into Eitri's own configuration is a card in Manual and Auto, also where Claude Code runs its own
  auto mode, and no saved rule allows it: `~/.config/eitri` (`init.lua`, `lua/`, `neovide.toml`), Eitri's data and
  state directories (your plugins, the saved rules, trusted projects, remembered modes) and the directory the program
  runs from. Until now Claude Code's auto mode decided these writes, and what was written took effect at the next
  start. Bypass still allows them. A shell command that reaches them without naming them is not caught
  (`docs/guide/permissions.md`, "Eitri's own configuration").

**Installing**

- The installer tries a download again when the connection breaks or stalls, and a file download picks up where it
  stopped: up to eight attempts in a row that receive nothing (20 in all), waiting 2 to 16 seconds between them. Every
  file is still checked against the signature or its pinned hash afterwards. npm is asked for five tries as well,
  unless your environment sets `npm_config_fetch_retries`. A download that still fails names the file, the host and what
  was tried, and points at `https_proxy` and `--base-url`. An attempt now gives up after a minute under 1 KiB/s (it was
  five minutes, with no second attempt), and a connection after 30 seconds.
- Each download can come from a mirror you name: `EITRI_RELEASE_BASE_URL` (or `--base-url`), `EITRI_SIDECAR_BASE_URL`,
  `EITRI_NVIM_BASE_URL`, `EITRI_NODE_BASE_URL`, `EITRI_GRPC_BASE_URL` and `EITRI_SKIA_BASE_URL`. Whatever the address,
  every file is still checked against the signed release, so a mirror that serves other bytes stops the install (unless
  you pass `--insecure-skip-signature`). An address must be `https://` (plain `http://` only to this machine) and may not
  carry a user name, password, query or fragment. npm already uses your own registry setting. A failed download names
  the variable that moves it. INSTALL.md lists them under "Download addresses".
- A cut-off `install.sh` runs nothing and exits non-zero wherever the cut falls after its first line; until now a cut
  inside its opening comment exited 0. A download that breaks before its first byte still exits 0 through the pipe:
  INSTALL.md shows a download-first line for scripts (`On a slow or filtered network`).
- On Ubuntu, the end of an install now says plainly that the agent panel stays off until the two printed `sudo`
  commands are run, and why. The installer still never runs `sudo`.
- This installer does not install 0.3.0 or older. Asked for one (`--version 0.3.0`, say), it stops before
  downloading anything and says to use that release's own `install.sh` (its release asset, or the file at its tag);
  until now it downloaded the release and then called it "not a valid Eitri release". `--from-source --checkout` is not
  affected.
- X11: GNOME on Xorg (Ubuntu 24.04) has been tried and works; other X11 sessions are untested (`docs/known-issues.md`).

**Sidecar updates, without a new Eitri**

- When a Claude Code update breaks the panel, we can publish a new sidecar (the agent backend) and you take it with
  `eitri sidecar update`; you do not wait for an Eitri release. `eitri sidecar status` shows what new tabs run,
  `eitri sidecar check` asks whether a newer release exists, and `eitri sidecar use bundled` goes back to the sidecar
  that came with this Eitri.
- Nothing checks by itself and the panel shows no notice. A sidecar release is signed source, so the update is built
  on your machine, as the first sidecar is: network access to nodejs.org, npm and grpc.io, and about 1.2 GiB of free
  disk space while it runs (one build we measured peaked at 1.15 GiB and took half a minute on a fast connection).
  The signature is checked first, with the same key as this release, and there is no way to skip it.
- A tab that is already open keeps the sidecar it started with; new tabs use the update. If an update will not start
  or lacks something this Eitri needs, the tab runs the bundled sidecar, shows `⚠` and says why.
- This starts with this release: 0.3.0 cannot take updates. After a `.deb` or `.rpm` upgrade run `eitri setup` once
  before `eitri sidecar update`, since the update needs the bundled sidecar built behind it.

**Plan mode and images in Claude tabs**

- Plan mode: `/plan` or `<leader>p` on an idle Claude tab, or on a new one before its first message. Claude reads and
  plans, and its plan comes up in a box in place of the message box with four choices: Keep planning (with optional
  feedback for Claude), Approve (Manual), Approve (Auto), and, only when plan mode started from Bypass, Approve and keep
  Bypass. The band shows `⏸ plan · from <mode>` while the tab plans. Claude can also enter plan mode by itself.
- While Claude Code plans, Eitri approves no file edit or command by itself, in any mode, Bypass included: Claude Code
  writes its own plan file, and every other `Write`, `Edit`, `NotebookEdit` or `Bash` call is a card. Only your choice
  in the plan box settles a plan; bypass, the card keys and saved rules never do.
- Images: paste an image into the box (`Ctrl+V`) or drop image files onto the conversation. PNG, JPEG, GIF (its first
  frame) and WebP; an image over 1568 pixels on its long side or 1.15 megapixels is scaled down to fit. The bundled
  agent backend takes up to 8 images and 3.5 MB of them in one message, and a draft holds up to those 8. A message with
  images always goes as a turn of its own, never into a running one, and never with a slash command or a `!` line.
- Claude Code keeps the images you send in the session file it writes on disk; Eitri does not remove them. After
  16 MB of images a tab says once that the conversation is heavy.
- Known limits: plan mode supervises Claude and is not a sandbox (your hooks still run, and MCP servers stay loaded);
  images are decoded in a separate process under memory and time limits, as you, not in a sandbox, and need Linux
  5.11 or newer. Codex and ACP tabs have neither plan mode nor images. Details in the known issues.

**Fixed**

- An idle Claude session's sidecar no longer grows. In 0.3.0 each one grew 30 to 47 MB an hour for as long as the tab
  stayed open. This release bundles sidecar 0.5.0, which carries the fix from sidecar 0.3.0: in the same 25-minute test
  of one idle session on sidecar 0.3.0, its memory held at 102.6 MB from the fifth minute, where Eitri 0.3.0's grew
  22 MB an hour (the JavaScript heap 44 MB an hour, against 0.3 MB an hour on sidecar 0.3.0). That test was not repeated on 0.5.0.
- Answering a permission card no longer freezes the window while the reply is sent, and a mode that could not be saved
  says so. A key you hold down never answers a card or follows a link hint.
- Fewer freezes and crashes: opening a path, reading a scratch draft and closing a window no longer wait on the disk on
  the GTK thread; terminal output can no longer exhaust memory or kill the shell, and a paste that times out is
  cancelled; a malformed subagent chain, clipboard text with a NUL and a cyclic layout no longer crash; and a stale
  instance sweep never deletes a live window's files.
- Turn review's revert never deletes a file that existed before the turn, and reverting one hunk follows later edits
  to the file. A removed line's tabs line up with the buffer in the drawing, and the editor's reload timer no longer
  opens a swap dialog or an E211 stall in your Neovim.
- A Codex or ACP agent whose stream breaks is stopped and a cancel is never lost; a Claude tab's steered messages keep
  their order; `!` commands no longer leave reader threads behind and say when their output is partial.
- Long conversations stream without the page's work growing with their length, and zooming the agent panel keeps your
  place. The status band stays one row, and a link opens on one click only when all of it is on screen.
- On Hyprland the editor no longer shows shifted up, and on a compositor that rounds window corners the editor's
  inner corners show its own colour instead of the desktop. In compositor mode, a burst of window closes keeps a
  group's tabs.
- Neovim's snippet and socket directories live only where no other user can swap them.

**What this can break:**

- **On Hyprland, sway and niri, chats and pages leave the Eitri window** and Eitri's layout keys go off: splits, zoom,
  floats, workspaces and the second window's keys do nothing there but show one hint. Add
  `eitri.config.set("window.tiling", "eitri")` to `init.lua` to keep the window as it was. Your layout file and tabs are
  kept for it either way.
- **In that mode, closing a chat window forgets its tabs,** with no question when it is idle. Closing the editor's
  window keeps them.
- **New default keys after the prefix: `F`, `S`, `_`, `W`, `O`, `u` and `B`.** If your `init.lua` already binds one
  with `eitri.keymap.set`, your binding wins and Eitri says so once at start, naming the key and the action it
  replaced; the new default is not on that key. A Lua panel or a plugin page whose module key is one of them
  loses the key, and the startup message names the key and what holds it.
- **On sway or i3 with Alt as `$mod`, the Alt keys after the prefix move to Ctrl+Alt** unless your `init.lua` touches
  any of them.
- **In a Bypass tab, the calls of a dynamic workflow's agents are now cards.** Claude Code does not show Eitri those
  calls start, so Eitri cannot tell whether one was made in plan mode; it waits 2 seconds and shows a card. In Manual
  and Auto tabs the same applies to their file edits and commands that a saved rule or Claude Code's auto mode would
  have handled. Every file edit and command, and in Bypass every call, now waits until Eitri sees Claude Code start
  it, usually about 0.2 seconds.
- **A bare `/plan` no longer goes to Claude Code as a message.** It switches plan mode in Eitri.
- **In companion mode on Hyprland, a key at a real edge now stops there.** Hyprland's own move picks a window even when
  nothing lies that way, so `Ctrl+k` and `Ctrl+j` could leave for the panel. Windows that overlap are still left to it.
- **snacks.nvim's smooth scrolling is off in the Neovim Eitri starts.** Set `vim.g.snacks_scroll = true` in your Neovim
  configuration to keep it.
- **In the Markdown preview, `j`, `k`, `d`, `u`, `Ctrl+f`, `gg` and `G` move the cursor, which the page follows,**
  where 0.3.0 scrolled the page; `Ctrl+e` and `Ctrl+y` still scroll. An `init.lua` override that names
  `scroll.half_down`, `scroll.half_up`, `scroll.page_down`, `scroll.top` or `scroll.bottom` is now ignored: name
  `cursor.half_down`, `cursor.half_up`, `cursor.page_down`, `cursor.first` or `cursor.last` instead. The 0.3.0 guide's
  example put `edit.here` on `e`; if you copied it, `e` keeps doing that instead of moving to a word's end.

## What's new in 0.3.0

0.3.0 is the tiling release. The window becomes a keyboard-driven tiling manager, Codex and any agent that speaks
ACP join Claude Code, and you can put web pages of your own in the window. A Claude Code tab now also shows what
Claude Code does on its own: background tasks, workflows, the turns it starts by itself and the questions it asks
you. Your Neovim stays the editor: a real `nvim --embed` with your own `init.lua`, plugins, LSP and keymaps.

**Your Neovim**

- Unchanged at the core: Eitri draws your own Neovim with a fork of Neovide's renderer and never replaces it. The
  editor is one module of the window, and every other module sits beside it.
- The window title names the project (`myproject — Eitri · docs`).

**Opening a file**

- `eitri FILE` opens a single file. The project comes from the file: its git repository, or its folder when there is
  none, or your current directory when that directory holds the file and lies inside the repository. Several
  files, `+N` and `+` before the first file, `FILE:LINE` and `FILE:LINE:COL`, `--` and `eitri DIR FILE` work as
  `nvim` users expect; `+page.svelte` and similar are refused as nvim commands.
- When a window of that project is already open, the file opens in it, the window comes to the front and the
  command returns at once. A window on a folder above the project worked out for the file counts too, up to the
  file's repository, nearest first. Typed in Eitri's own bottom terminal, it opens in that window. `--new-window`
  always starts a new window; so do `eitri DIR`, `--clean` and `--account`.
- A file with no project of its own (`eitri ~/notes.md`, `eitri /etc/hosts`, `eitri /tmp/x.py`) opens in its folder
  with no project boundary: a Claude tab asks before every file and command, a Codex tab starts read-only, and the
  panel says so in a note.
- On GNOME a command typed in a terminal cannot raise the window (GNOME wants an activation token it does not
  have), so GNOME may only show a "ready" notice. Only sway was tried; the raise on GNOME has not been.
- Eitri now appears under Open With for plain text, Markdown, C, C++, Python, Rust, JSON, TOML and shell scripts
  (it does not become the default); that listing has not been tried in a file manager. `eitri split FILE` and
  `eitri panel FILE` are not supported: both still take a directory.

**Keyboard-driven tiling**

- Splits, tabbed and stacked containers (`<prefix> Alt+w` / `Alt+s`), floats (`<prefix> Alt+Shift+Space`), a
  scratchpad (`<prefix> Alt+_` puts a module away, `Alt+-` brings it back) and named workspaces (`<prefix> (`, `)`,
  `L`, `s`, `$`; `main` always stays). i3's moves are there as Alt chords after the prefix.
- tmux's prefix keys, with `Ctrl+b` as the prefix and 36 new keys after it: layouts (`Alt+3` to `Alt+5`, `Space`,
  `E`), rotate (`Ctrl+o`, `Alt+o`), mark and swap (`m`, `M`), move to an edge (`H`, `J`, `K`), pane numbers (`q`),
  moving a module (`Alt+H/J/K/L`), selecting a container (`Alt+a`, `Alt+d`), breaking a chat tab out into a view of
  its own (`!`), and each pane's own text size (`Ctrl+=` / `Ctrl+-` / `Ctrl+0`). With `keymap.from_tmux` on, Eitri
  reads more of your `~/.tmux.conf` than before: the layout commands, `rotate-window`, `display-panes`,
  `break-pane`, `switch-client -n/-p/-l`, `choose-tree -s`, `select-pane -m/-M`, `swap-pane` and
  `split-window -c "#{pane_current_path}"`.
- Up to nine terminals (`<prefix> Alt+Enter`), each with its own shell, and up to nine chat views side by side.
- In Lua: `eitri.layout.default`, `.workspace`, `.rule` (where a new module opens), `.open`, `.float`, `.run`, and
  `eitri.layout.export()`, which returns the window you arranged as the `init.lua` calls that make it.
- The layout file is now version 2. The old file is read once and left as it was, so going back to 0.2.2 is safe.
- For i3 or sway with `$mod = Alt`, `docs/keymap/i3-alt-mod.lua` moves the rows your window manager would take.
- `:EitriTutor` in the editor teaches the window's keys hands-on, in English or Simplified Chinese, on Neovim's own
  tutor. The Keys page of the guide now opens with the prefix basics.

**Pages you build** (experimental)

- A Markdown preview ships with Eitri, off until you add `eitri.plugin.load("markdown")` to `init.lua`. It
  renders as you type (GitHub-flavoured Markdown, KaTeX, Mermaid, alerts) and scrolls in step with Neovim both
  ways. `https` images in a document load unless you set `remote_images = "off"`.
- Your own pages: a Lua panel (`eitri.panel.register`) shows an HTML file from `~/.config/eitri/panels/`, or any
  address such as your development server, as a module of the window. A plugin is a directory with an
  `eitri-plugin.toml`; its page gets only the host powers the manifest lists, Eitri's colours and the shared reading
  keys (scrolling, find, hints, history and Vim-style visual selection with `v`/`V`; the Markdown preview adds a
  cursor of its own, and `Enter` on a selection hands it to Neovim, selected there). The guide's new "Pages you build" page shows both, with a complete small plugin.
- **Experimental:** the manifest and the page bridge may change during 0.x, and there is no consent screen yet:
  loading a plugin trusts it as you trust any program you run.

**Agents you supervise**

- Claude Code tabs request Claude Code's own system prompt (the one `claude -p` uses), plus a short
  note about Eitri's panel; **Codex tabs** (`:tabnew codex`, with `codex` 0.160.0 or newer, always in Codex's
  `workspace-write` sandbox); and **any ACP agent** you register with `eitri.agent.register{...}`. Each kind asks its
  own trust question about the project files it would load.
- **Manual mode**: in a Claude Code tab every file edit is a card you answer, and your saved rules still apply.
  In a Codex tab, Manual makes each request to leave Codex's sandbox a card, while edits and commands inside the
  sandbox still run without asking (the agents guide says more); ACP tabs have no Manual. `Shift+Tab` cycles
  Manual, Auto and Bypass; `agent.default_mode` sets where a new tab starts.
- **Enter while a turn runs steers it** in Claude Code and Codex tabs: the agent reads your message at its next
  step. An ACP tab holds the message until the turn ends. `Ctrl+Enter` interrupts the turn and sends at once.
- The chat page is redone: one quiet column, frames only around what you review or answer, the agent's thinking
  shown as you choose (`agent.thinking`), `Worked for 8s` under each finished turn, and a `/` menu with the
  agent's own commands.
- Turn review counts Codex and ACP edits, reverts a single hunk by checking only that hunk's lines, and `y` on a
  file row copies its path.
- The trust question shows everything whole, and also covers `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json` and rules in the
  directories above the repository.

**Claude Code's own work, in the tab**

- A turn Claude Code starts without you is shown like yours. When a background command finishes, a monitor
  reports, a subagent or workflow ends, or a scheduled wake-up fires, the tab shows what started the turn
  (`Background command "build" completed`, `Scheduled wake-up: <reason>`), then its tool calls, cards and reply.
  A message you send while it runs goes into that turn, and one Claude Code keeps for later waits as a `↪` line
  until it is read.
- Background work has a list. The band counts it in Claude Code's words (`2 shells, 1 monitor`), and `/tasks`
  lists every background shell, monitor, subagent and workflow: `Enter` shows a task's detail and the end of its
  output, `x` stops that one task, and `o` opens its output read-only in Neovim.
- When a dynamic workflow needs your approval, its card shows the name, arguments and script (`View raw script`
  opens it in Neovim). While one runs, the band shows how many of its agents are done, and `/workflows` lists its
  phases and agents.
- A foreground subagent's calls are nested under its row; a background subagent takes one status line, and its
  report starts a turn of its own.
- Claude Code's notices appear where its terminal shows them: `Compacting conversation...` and then a divider,
  the retry while the model service is overloaded, and the words of a `UserPromptSubmit` hook that blocked a prompt.
- `/clear` starts a new conversation in the same tab. Background tasks keep running, and a resume continues the
  new conversation.
- When Claude asks you a multiple-choice question (`AskUserQuestion`), a question box takes the place of the message
  box. You pick with the number keys or `j`/`k`, can type an answer of your own (`Ctrl+g` edits it in Neovim), add
  notes to an option with a preview, and send with `Ctrl+Enter`. Only you answer: bypass, `Ctrl+y`, the card keys
  and saved rules never touch a question.
- A resumed conversation follows Claude Code's own turns: turns it started by itself come back with their cause,
  the `/` and `!` commands you typed come back as your prompts, and questions come back with your answers.
- Eitri raises Claude Code's longest allowed command timeout (`BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS`) to 2147483647 ms, so a
  background command Claude starts with a long timeout keeps running. A background command started without a
  timeout is still stopped after 30 minutes, Claude Code's own default, which Eitri keeps on purpose; a value you
  set yourself wins.
- Not in this release: plan mode, attaching files or images to a message, the terminal's `Ctrl+B` that moves a
  running command to the background (in Eitri `Ctrl+b` is the prefix), and a list of scheduled wake-ups and cron
  tasks. Those still fire, each as a turn of its own, while the tab stays open; closing the tab ends them, except a
  cron task Claude Code was told to keep across sessions, which it saves in the project and may run in a later
  session there.

The agents guide's first section describes all of this.

**Lighter at rest**

- An idle window draws nothing, and the panel wakes only when it has something to draw. The bottom terminal echoes
  a key without waiting for a frame, and a long scrollback costs far less to draw.

**Companion mode on Hyprland**

- Focus moves and raises now work on Hyprland's Lua config (0.55 and later) and on 0.57, which removed the legacy
  `hyprctl dispatch` names Eitri sent: `Ctrl+h/j/k/l` at the panel's edge, nvim's edge key toward the panel,
  `:EitriPanel` and the hand-back to the editor did nothing there. Eitri now asks Hyprland once which config it
  runs and writes its commands to match, and a command Hyprland refuses is written to the log once. Run on a live
  Hyprland 0.57.0, and 0.56.2 with `hyprland.conf` and with `hyprland.lua`. The second window's raises have not
  been run on a live Hyprland.

**What this can break:**

- **A key of yours over one of the new prefix keys takes it, and the new action is not on that key.** Eitri says so
  once at start, naming the key and the action it replaced. The new keys, in the spelling `eitri.keymap` takes: `m`,
  `M`, `q`, `E`, `H`, `J`, `K`, `L`, `s`, `Space`, `(`, `)`, `$`, `!`, `C-o`, `C-=`, `C-+`, `C--`, `C-0`, `M-3`, `M-4`,
  `M-5`, `M-e`, `M-o`, `M-w`, `M-s`, `M-a`, `M-d`, `M-H`, `M-J`, `M-K`, `M-L`, `M-Enter`, `M-S-Space`, `M--` and `M-_`.
  A `eitri.keymap.del("prefix", "<key>")` before your `set` still works and says nothing, and with the tmux import on
  and your tmux configuration binding the key you need no `del`. A `del` of a key that nothing is bound to is left out
  and listed at start; the rest of your keys apply. A Lua panel or plugin keyed `H`, `J` or `K` loses its key.
- **`init.lua` is read from `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eitri`** when that variable is set to an absolute path, and from
  `~/.config/eitri` otherwise (`EITRI_CONFIG_DIR` still comes first); 0.2.2 read `~/.config/eitri` whatever
  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` said. If your `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` points elsewhere, move your configuration there.
  `EITRI_CONFIG_DIR` must now be an absolute path: a relative one stops startup, and an empty one counts as unset.
- **`Shift+Tab` cycles three modes**: Manual, Auto, Bypass, then Manual again. Bypass is entered only from Auto,
  so leaving it lands in Manual (in Auto in an ACP tab, which has no Manual).
- **Every project you trusted asks once more**, because the question now shows more than it did.
- **In the message box, `Ctrl+a` and `Ctrl+e` move to the start and end of the line**, as in a shell; they no
  longer select everything.
- The panel's typing mode is now called INSERT (it was INPUT).
- **In a Claude Code tab, `Ctrl+c` no longer stops background work.** It interrupts the turn; background commands,
  subagents and workflows keep running. Stop one from `/tasks` with `x`.
- **Closing a Claude Code tab, a chat view or the window asks first while background tasks run** or a question
  waits, and says what would stop. Scheduled wake-ups and cron tasks are not counted: closing ends them without
  asking, apart from a cron task Claude Code was told to keep across sessions.
- **In a Manual tab, Claude Code's scheduling and task tools no longer ask** (`ScheduleWakeup`, the cron tools,
  `ListAgents`, `TaskStop`); `SendMessage` still does.
- **A folder that is no boundary now makes a Claude tab ask, however it was opened.** That is your home directory
  or above it, a folder you do not own, a folder other users can write to, the filesystem root and the kernel
  trees. `eitri DIR` on a tree others can write to, on one you do not own (another user's checkout, a container
  running as root over a mounted checkout, some network mounts), or on a 0775 checkout whose group Eitri can find
  only through NSS (not in `/etc/group`), used to run as a project and now asks before every file and command.
  A checkout that is `drwxrwxr-x you you` with a group that only you belong to is unchanged. The panel shows a note
  that says why.
- **An Auto tab at a home directory now asks.** Where Claude Code runs its own auto mode, an Auto tab used to leave
  every call to Claude Code's classifier even at `eitri ~`; at a folder that is no boundary Eitri's own rules answer
  instead, as in a Manual tab, and put each call that is judged against the project on a card. Bypass is unchanged.
- **A Codex tab at such a folder is now read-only, `eitri ~` included.** It used to keep `workspace-write` over the
  whole folder, so it could write anywhere in it with no card. Now a write has to ask, and in an Auto tab the request
  is a card too: Codex's own reviewer does not decide there. Bypass still approves it. The project's own Codex
  configuration does not load there, even after a `y` to the trust question. A tab of an agent you registered keeps
  its own rules unless its registration names the new `read_only_mode`.
- **`eitri FILE` when a window of the project is open hands the file to that window** instead of starting one;
  `--new-window` is the way to get a new window.
- **Cards are answered in card mode.** `a`, `d` and `D` in BROWSE, `]p` and `[p`, and `Ctrl+y` in the box answer nothing
  now; they say where the card keys are. Enter card mode with `Alt-n` or `Enter` on the card, then answer with `1`, `2`
  or `3`, or `h`/`l` and `Enter`. A click on a button still answers. The `y/n` questions Eitri asks (entering Bypass,
  stopping a turn, closing a tab) keep `y` and `n`.
- **`Ctrl+o` in BROWSE goes back through your jumps** and no longer shows the detailed view: that is `zi`. The message
  box keeps `Ctrl+o` for it.
- **`eitri` in a terminal returns the prompt at once and prints nothing.** The window runs on its own, so closing the
  terminal no longer ends it, and its output goes to a log. `eitri --no-fork` is the old way.
- **Starting Eitri from the desktop shows the project list** instead of opening your home folder; set
  `window.desktop_start = "cwd"` for the old behaviour.
- **Agents get editor tools by default,** in every kind of tab, and can read your open buffers with unsaved changes;
  `agent.editor.tools = false` turns them off. Writes through them are cards in Manual and Auto.
- **A Neovim older than 0.11 no longer runs as Eitri's editor.** The installer and `eitri setup` offer a private 0.12.6.
  Moving a terminal Neovim in with `:EitriOpen` needs 0.12 on that Neovim.
- **Eitri reopens your files, splits and tabs** at the next start in a project. `editor.restore = false` turns it off,
  and a session plugin you use makes Eitri stand aside.
- **A tmux `bind v ...` after the prefix now takes effect** when `keymap.from_tmux` is on. It used to be skipped.
- **A mistyped path can open a window.** `eitri typo` used to stop with `no such directory`. In a folder that exists
  it now opens an empty buffer named `typo`, with the project found from that folder, and `:w` creates the file. A
  path in a folder that does not exist still stops the launch.
- **A second Eitri window on the same project keeps its own layout and saved tabs.** It starts from the first
  window's layout and then saves only its own, so closing it no longer changes what the first window opens with.

## Upgrading from 0.2.2

- **The agent backend is built again, once.** 0.3.0 pins a newer Verdandi, so `install.sh`
  builds the new backend before it switches over, and after a `.deb`/`.rpm` upgrade you run `eitri setup` before
  your first turn. It downloads Node.js and the Claude Agent SDK from npm again (about 1.4 GiB of free disk while it builds).
- A Claude Code conversation started in Eitri before 0.3.0 **keeps** its earlier, shorter instructions when you
  continue it (measured with Claude Code 2.1.302); a new conversation gets the full ones, and `/compact` may
  refresh them too (not yet measured).
- Your window opens as you left it. Its 0.2.2 layout file is read once into the new one and left as it was, so
  0.2.2 still opens with it.

**`v0.2.0` will stop building from git.** The public history of the Neovide fork was rewritten after 0.2.0
(only commit metadata changed; the files are the same), so the `v0.2.0` tag and every earlier public commit
point at fork commits that no branch holds any more. Build 0.2.1 or later from git. For 0.2.0 itself, its
own `install.sh` (the 0.2.0 release asset) with `--from-source` works only until GitHub stops serving those
old objects, and its `eitri-0.2.0-source.tar.gz` release asset, which carries the fork's files, rebuilds it
for good; later releases' `install.sh` cannot build 0.2.0 from source. The 0.2.0 packages, tarball and
`install.sh` are not affected.

## How much of this has been tried

Everything new has automated tests. The tiling window (containers, floats, the scratchpad, workspaces, chat views and
open rules) and the Markdown preview passed every row of their checks. On this release's backend, the real Claude Code
2.1.322 ran against the model service on 2026-11-20: all seven session lifecycle checks (among them a second turn, an
interrupt, a tool run once allowed, a resume, and no sidecar or `claude` process left behind) and all five permission
checks (allow, deny, an interrupt and a closed session while a request waits, an edit under Auto) passed, as did plan
mode and images (below). The wider suites that run the real `claude` against the model service (2.1.302; 113 cases)
last ran on a development build with an earlier Verdandi than the one this release pins. They ran in two sessions
because the first stopped when the test account reached the model service's rate limit; after the second, all 113
pass. Two cases failed along the way for reasons other than this release's change: one hit the model service's
usage-policy check on a probe that asks for a destructive command (it passed when run again), and one met this CLI
version's new transcript line types, which the history reader now skips. Eight Glob and Grep probes in one suite are
skipped, because this CLI build lists no such tools. The request a Claude Code tab sends was captured from that same
earlier backend against a local stand-in: it carries `claude -p`'s system prompt with Claude Code's own identity line
for the Agent SDK and Eitri's note once at its end. Codex tabs passed their scripted suites with `codex` 0.160.0 to
0.161.0 (a newer Codex runs and is marked untested), and the real `codex` 0.162 and 0.163 were run against a scripted
model. `eitri FILE` passed 46 checks on sway, with the real launcher, a scripted Claude Code stand-in and the real
`codex` against a scripted model: a file in a repository, `+42`, `~/notes.md` with its note and a card in a Manual and
in an Auto tab, a Codex tab and a registered agent at such a folder, a file handed to a running window from outside,
through a folder above its project, and from its bottom terminal, a window that cannot take it, and a saved layout
that hides the editor. Read-only at such a folder was run against `codex` 0.160.0, 0.162.1, 0.163.0 and 0.164.0.
GNOME's raise and a scaled output have not been tried. Companion mode ran on Hyprland 0.57.0 and 0.56.2; on GNOME only
the extension's moves were tried, and it has not been tried on niri or KDE yet.

The Claude Code tab's handling of Claude Code's own work was tested on this release's backend with the real Claude
Code 2.1.311 against a scripted stand-in for the model service. The flows above ran end to end through Eitri's agent
host, and fifteen screen checks of them passed in the redesigned chat page, in dark and light and at a narrow panel
width: turns Claude Code started, `/tasks` and `/workflows`, the workflow card, subagents, compaction, a message
steered into a running turn, `/clear`, a scheduled wake-up, retries and the question box, where an answer typed
through an input method was sent. Restoring a resumed conversation was also compared with real Claude Code session
files. Reopening a saved chat was run against the model service with the real Claude Code 2.1.322: the earlier
messages show while the tab starts, the tab then takes another turn, and a session Claude Code no longer has ends in a
message saying so. Steering was checked on screen only in a Claude Code tab, and typing through an input method into
the companion panel, in its message box or its question box, has not been verified.

Plan mode and image attachments were tested with the real Claude Code 2.1.311 against a scripted stand-in for the
model service, end to end through Eitri's agent host: an approval into each of the three modes, keep planning with
feedback and a second plan, entering and leaving plan mode yourself, interrupting or closing the tab while a plan
waits, file edits and commands while Claude plans, and turns with one or four images, images alone and images queued
behind a running turn. The plan box and the image chips then passed their screen checks, in dark and light, at a
normal width, at a 320-pixel chat pane and in a full-width companion panel: all three approvals, keep planning, a plan
waiting through an interrupt, a closed tab, a stopped agent backend and a page reload, a hostile plan drawn inert,
pasted and dropped images, their errors, and images queued, taken back and carried across a tab switch and a reload.
Against the model service, the real Claude Code 2.1.322 on this release's backend planned, revised a plan and carried
it out after an approval, held its edit for an answer after a plan approved into Manual, and read an attached
image; 2.1.311 had done the same on earlier builds.

The other work since 0.3.0 was checked with Neovim 0.12.6 and scripted agents. Taking over a terminal Neovim passed
all 17 of its rows, each of the five ways of closing the window among them. Pages beside your own Neovim passed 60 of
60 checks. Reopening your files passed its seven window rows, and its record was run on Neovim 0.10.4, 0.11.2 and
0.12.6 and with six starter configurations. The project list and the folder browser, the agents pages (after one fix
a first pass found), crash records (60 of 60 rows) and the Markdown diagram viewer (16 of 16) passed their rows too.
Live review passed the machine checks of its 14 rows; one check was marked failed by a host-wide test in four runs in
a row, and its cause is not established. The launcher change (the prompt back at once, the login shell's `PATH`) has
not been tried from a desktop launcher yet. The editor tools ran against a fake language server and a scripted model,
and the `claude` in your terminal against a stand-in client, not a real interactive Claude Code.

0.4.0 is an early release and not yet stable: expect rough edges, and the `init.lua` API and the default keys may
still change during 0.x. What you may run into is listed in the known issues (`docs/known-issues.md` in the
repository).

## Performance, against native Neovide

Measured on development builds shortly before 0.3.0 unless a bullet says otherwise, on an AMD desktop, and on an
Intel laptop where it says so; not repeated for this release:

- **Drawn the GTK way, typing on GTK 4.24 is one refresh slower than native Neovide.** That is no longer the default
  there (the editor's own surface, above); it still applies on X11, with NVIDIA's driver and with
  `editor.presentation = "gtk"`. With the compositor feeding GTK a real
  165 Hz period through presentation feedback, GTK 4.24.1 added 5.97 ms at the median; the same build with
  GTK 4.22.5 added 0.30 ms. With GTK's 60 Hz fallback clock, the addition was about 16.5 ms. A 156-line
  GTK-only reproduction also shows the delay on GTK's development branch (4.25.0,
  commit c36202f6, 2026-10-25). Three workarounds using public GTK API were measured; none restored native
  latency, so none ships. The one measured at the real 165 Hz period (`render`) still added about 5.8 ms;
  the other two were measured only with the 60 Hz fallback clock. Settled idle was measured only with
  that fallback clock and stayed quiet.
- **Drawn the GTK way on GTK 4.24, scrolling with a held `j` at a 165 Hz output drops frames.** Native Neovide drew
  165.5 changed frames a second, Eitri on GTK 4.24.1 140.6, and the same build on GTK 4.22.5 165.4.
  The 99th-percentile frame intervals were 6.1, 12.1 and 6.1 ms respectively. This separate scrolling run
  used stock presentation feedback, so GTK 4.24 still used its 60 Hz fallback clock; scrolling has not been
  repeated with real-period feedback. With the editor's own surface, a held-key test did no worse than the GTK way.
- **While a reply streams**, a key's redraw took 11.2 ms at the median with WebKitGTK 2.54 (a stand-in reply),
  against about 2.3 ms with WebKitGTK 2.52.
- **Memory** (a development build after 0.3.0): an idle window with one tab used 232 MiB more than native Neovide
  with the panel inside the window and 245 MiB more in compositor mode, against a goal of 256 MiB; on an integrated
  GPU, 248 to 259 MiB more.
- **Idle:** no frames drawn, about 13 ms of CPU a minute. **Startup:** 260 ms, against native's 253 ms.
- **Under load at high refresh rates** the Intel laptop drew 86-93 % of native Neovide's frames at 165 Hz while
  other work kept it 17-28 % busy. That was measured before 0.2.0's typing-latency fix and not since.

## Install

    curl --proto '=https' --proto-redir '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSfL https://github.com/HunterGrey-cyber/eitri/releases/download/v@VERSION@/install.sh | sh -s -- --version @VERSION@

or install `eitri_@VERSION@_amd64.deb` / `eitri-@VERSION@-1.x86_64.rpm` and run `eitri setup`
once, or install from `eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz` with
`sh install.sh --tarball eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz --sums SHA256SUMS --sig SHA256SUMS.sig`
(or unpack it and run its own `bin/eitri setup` from inside the extracted directory). A `.deb`/`.rpm` upgrade
from an earlier release needs that `eitri setup` again: it builds the new agent backend.

Eitri needs `nvim` >= 0.11 somewhere it can find one -- either on `PATH`, or its own private copy
under `XDG_DATA_HOME/eitri/nvim` (never placed on `PATH`; none of the packages depend on a system
nvim). Ubuntu 24.04's own package is 0.9.5, below that floor. `install.sh` (the curl command above)
offers to fetch the official nvim release for you at install time if none new enough is found; run
`eitri setup --nvim-only` on its own at any later time -- after a `.deb`/`.rpm`/tarball install,
or again -- to get the same offer.

## The agent backend is built on your machine

No asset here contains Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK. `eitri setup` downloads it from npm and
builds the agent backend (the sidecar) on your own machine, from `@VERDANDI_ASSET@` (the public
Verdandi at @VERDANDI_REV@) and the official Node release pinned in `RELEASE`. The SDK is not open
source; its own licence applies to your copy of it, and `eitri setup` shows where to read it.

## Licences and source

This program statically links nvim-rs @NVIM_RS_VERSION@, which is licensed under the GNU LGPL-3.0;
its use is covered by that licence. The Corresponding Source for this release is
`@SOURCE_ASSET@` on this page -- **not** GitHub's automatic "Source code" archives, which lack the
Neovide fork submodule, the vendored Rust dependencies and the Skia archive the build linked. It
rebuilds offline, and `SOURCE` (installed at `share/licenses/eitri/SOURCE`) gives the exact recipe
for relinking against a modified nvim-rs. `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES`, beside it, carries every other
notice.

